WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
A loaf of bread, a merino's fine wool,

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.160
and a vial of human insulin.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.680
All three come from biotechnology.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.160
But only one of them needed anyone to

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.960
change an organism's DNA in a lab, and

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.680
people were baking with yeast thousands of years

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.480
before anyone knew DNA existed.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.200
So what counts as biotechnology, and what does

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:23.120
each kind actually change?

00:00:24.334 --> 00:00:25.453
Start with the bread.

00:00:26.014 --> 00:00:28.413
Yeast is a single-celled fungus.

00:00:28.654 --> 00:00:31.373
Mixed into dough, its cells break down sugar

00:00:31.454 --> 00:00:34.973
for energy, releasing carbon dioxide, whose bubbles make

00:00:35.014 --> 00:00:38.013
the dough rise, and a little ethanol.

00:00:38.174 --> 00:00:40.093
People were baking and brewing this way for

00:00:40.174 --> 00:00:43.533
thousands of years, long before Louis Pasteur showed,

00:00:43.574 --> 00:00:46.493
in the eighteen-fifties and sixties, that living yeast

00:00:46.654 --> 00:00:48.013
does the work.

00:00:48.094 --> 00:00:51.213
Making bread doesn't change the yeast's DNA.

00:00:51.294 --> 00:00:53.613
We just use what its cells already do.

00:00:54.834 --> 00:00:56.153
That's the heart of it.

00:00:56.434 --> 00:01:00.033
Biotechnology is using living things, their cells or

00:01:00.114 --> 00:01:03.233
their processes, on purpose, to make a product

00:01:03.314 --> 00:01:04.593
or solve a problem.

00:01:04.914 --> 00:01:07.633
Bacteria turning milk into yoghurt count.

00:01:07.714 --> 00:01:11.073
So do microbes making enzymes for washing powders.

00:01:11.154 --> 00:01:13.633
Nothing in that definition says the DNA has

00:01:13.674 --> 00:01:15.953
to be changed, so a method can be

00:01:16.034 --> 00:01:19.153
thousands of years old and still be biotechnology.

00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:21.246
Now the wool.

00:01:21.327 --> 00:01:24.446
For generations, breeders kept the sheep with the

00:01:24.527 --> 00:01:26.766
finest wool and bred them together.

00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:29.166
That's selective breeding.

00:01:29.287 --> 00:01:31.406
No one touched a sheep's DNA.

00:01:31.487 --> 00:01:33.406
Each lamb still got a normal mix of

00:01:33.487 --> 00:01:35.086
its parents' alleles.

00:01:35.127 --> 00:01:37.966
But by choosing the parents, breeders changed which

00:01:38.047 --> 00:01:40.846
alleles were passed on, so alleles for finer

00:01:41.007 --> 00:01:43.566
wool became more common in the flock.

00:01:43.647 --> 00:01:46.366
It's slow, and it's limited to variation that

00:01:46.607 --> 00:01:48.126
already exists in the flock.

00:01:49.334 --> 00:01:50.613
Then the insulin.

00:01:50.894 --> 00:01:54.293
People with type one diabetes need insulin every

00:01:54.454 --> 00:01:54.693
day.

00:01:55.254 --> 00:01:57.813
From the nineteen-twenties, it was extracted from the

00:01:57.934 --> 00:02:00.373
pancreases of pigs and cattle.

00:02:00.534 --> 00:02:03.973
Then, in the early nineteen-eighties, human insulin made

00:02:04.054 --> 00:02:05.893
by bacteria went on sale.

00:02:06.614 --> 00:02:09.573
Scientists had put DNA coding for human insulin

00:02:09.654 --> 00:02:12.053
into the bacteria, which read it like any

00:02:12.134 --> 00:02:15.493
of their own genes, and made human insulin.

00:02:15.694 --> 00:02:19.413
Here the organism's DNA really was changed.

00:02:19.534 --> 00:02:22.293
Joining DNA from different sources like this is

00:02:22.414 --> 00:02:24.213
called recombinant DNA.

00:02:25.434 --> 00:02:27.913
So the useful question isn't whether a technique

00:02:27.994 --> 00:02:29.193
is old or new.

00:02:29.594 --> 00:02:32.153
It's what exactly is being changed.

00:02:32.474 --> 00:02:35.593
Some technologies just use an organism's process, like

00:02:35.674 --> 00:02:36.793
fermentation.

00:02:37.114 --> 00:02:40.713
Some choose which parents reproduce: selective breeding, and

00:02:40.914 --> 00:02:45.033
today, artificial insemination of livestock and artificial pollination

00:02:45.074 --> 00:02:46.033
of crops.

00:02:46.394 --> 00:02:49.913
Some copy existing DNA, growing identical plants from

00:02:49.994 --> 00:02:52.713
tissue samples, or making many copies of one

00:02:52.794 --> 00:02:53.273
gene.

00:02:53.754 --> 00:02:56.633
And some insert or edit DNA, like the

00:02:56.754 --> 00:02:59.593
insulin bacteria, and cotton carrying a gene from

00:02:59.674 --> 00:03:00.953
a soil bacterium.

00:03:02.167 --> 00:03:04.446
Each kind has its own advantage.

00:03:04.727 --> 00:03:07.446
Choosing parents works for traits controlled by many

00:03:07.527 --> 00:03:10.726
genes, like wool quality, because breeders judge the

00:03:10.807 --> 00:03:12.886
whole animal and don't need to know which

00:03:12.967 --> 00:03:14.326
genes are involved.

00:03:14.647 --> 00:03:17.286
But it uses only alleles the species already

00:03:17.367 --> 00:03:20.446
has, and it takes generations.

00:03:20.807 --> 00:03:24.166
Copying keeps a valuable combination of alleles together,

00:03:24.247 --> 00:03:26.486
with no reshuffling by meiosis.

00:03:26.727 --> 00:03:28.966
And inserting DNA can add a trait the

00:03:29.047 --> 00:03:32.486
species never had, in a single generation, like

00:03:32.527 --> 00:03:34.886
cotton plants that make their own protein against

00:03:34.967 --> 00:03:37.526
caterpillars, from a gene taken from a soil

00:03:37.567 --> 00:03:38.406
bacterium.

00:03:39.634 --> 00:03:40.913
Put it on a timeline.

00:03:41.474 --> 00:03:43.553
The old methods didn't disappear when the new

00:03:43.634 --> 00:03:44.513
ones arrived.

00:03:44.994 --> 00:03:48.673
Yeast still raises bread, and breeders still select,

00:03:48.754 --> 00:03:51.233
now helped by DNA tests that show which

00:03:51.314 --> 00:03:53.513
animals carry useful alleles.

00:03:53.954 --> 00:03:56.913
Sometimes one product spans the whole history.

00:03:57.474 --> 00:04:01.153
Cheesemakers traditionally used rennet, an enzyme taken from

00:04:01.234 --> 00:04:02.273
calves' stomachs.

00:04:02.754 --> 00:04:05.153
Today, much cheese is made with the same

00:04:05.394 --> 00:04:08.833
enzyme, chymosin, produced by microbes carrying the gene

00:04:08.914 --> 00:04:09.313
for it.

00:04:10.034 --> 00:04:14.353
Biotechnology works across agriculture, medicine and industry.

00:04:14.594 --> 00:04:17.393
And the newest tools, like gene editing, aim

00:04:17.474 --> 00:04:20.513
to change one chosen stretch of DNA, which

00:04:20.554 --> 00:04:22.353
is where future uses are heading.

00:04:23.567 --> 00:04:25.086
Two things to hold on to.

00:04:25.647 --> 00:04:29.966
Biotechnology is older than GMOs: bread, cheese and

00:04:30.047 --> 00:04:31.886
selective breeding all count.

00:04:32.287 --> 00:04:36.366
And reproductive technologies choose the parents; they don't

00:04:36.447 --> 00:04:37.406
change genes.

00:04:37.807 --> 00:04:40.206
They change which alleles get passed on, but

00:04:40.287 --> 00:04:42.126
they make no new ones.

00:04:42.167 --> 00:04:44.766
So judge a technology by what it changes,

00:04:44.847 --> 00:04:46.526
not by how modern it looks.

00:04:47.734 --> 00:04:49.453
Two exam questions now.

00:04:49.734 --> 00:04:50.213
First.

00:04:50.774 --> 00:04:54.613
For each example, identify what is being changed:

00:04:54.694 --> 00:04:57.573
an organism's process being used, the choice of

00:04:57.654 --> 00:05:01.413
parents, a copy of existing DNA, or the

00:05:01.494 --> 00:05:02.613
DNA itself.

00:05:03.494 --> 00:05:05.893
Bacteria turning milk into yoghurt.

00:05:06.534 --> 00:05:09.253
Semen from one prize bull used to inseminate

00:05:09.414 --> 00:05:10.613
hundreds of cows.

00:05:11.094 --> 00:05:13.733
Banana plants grown from suckers cut from one

00:05:13.814 --> 00:05:14.853
parent plant.

00:05:15.094 --> 00:05:17.973
And cotton carrying a gene from a soil

00:05:18.054 --> 00:05:18.773
bacterium.

00:05:19.494 --> 00:05:22.293
Then state which of these change an organism's

00:05:22.374 --> 00:05:23.493
DNA sequence.

00:05:23.974 --> 00:05:25.253
Pause here to answer.

00:05:35.667 --> 00:05:38.186
Yoghurt uses the bacteria's process.

00:05:38.387 --> 00:05:42.066
Insemination chooses the parents: each calf still gets

00:05:42.107 --> 00:05:43.786
a normal mix of alleles.

00:05:44.067 --> 00:05:46.226
Banana suckers are copies of the parent.

00:05:46.707 --> 00:05:49.186
Only the cotton has had its DNA changed,

00:05:49.267 --> 00:05:50.546
by an inserted gene.

00:05:51.107 --> 00:05:54.866
Saying artificial insemination changes the calves' DNA is

00:05:54.907 --> 00:05:56.306
the classic mistake.

00:05:57.534 --> 00:05:58.013
Second.

00:05:58.094 --> 00:06:01.533
Plant breeders want cotton plants that resist caterpillar

00:06:01.614 --> 00:06:02.173
pests.

00:06:02.734 --> 00:06:06.493
Compare selective breeding and recombinant DNA technology as

00:06:06.654 --> 00:06:07.613
ways to produce them.

00:06:08.174 --> 00:06:09.693
Pause here to answer.

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:22.300
Both use biology to get a trait that's

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:23.260
inherited.

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:26.580
Selective breeding chooses parents, so it can only

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:30.340
combine resistance alleles cotton already has, and it

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:32.140
takes many generations.

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:35.540
Recombinant DNA inserts a gene, even from another

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:38.180
species, so a new trait can appear in

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:39.780
one generation.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:43.460
Saying selective breeding isn't biotechnology is the classic

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:44.180
mistake.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:48.680
So when a question names a biotechnology, ask

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:49.880
what it changes.

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:52.920
Is an organism's process being used?

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:54.840
Are the parents being chosen?

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:57.320
Is existing DNA being copied?

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:00.520
Or is the DNA itself being changed?

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:03.480
Then give its use and its advantage.

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:06.280
What a technology changes also decides what it

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:08.840
can do to a population, which matters when

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:10.040
we judge it.

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:13.480
Next, how plant and animal biotechnology should be

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:14.200
judged.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:16.840
Here are the key notes.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:19.000
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:19.880
to copy them.
